# Git and production safety

This directory may not be a git repository yet. Do not run `git init` unless the developer explicitly authorizes it. Do not add remotes or rewrite history.

Prefer small, understandable commits when committing is requested. Never `git push --force`, `git reset --hard`, or `git clean -fd` without explicit authorization.

Never assume development access implies production access. Do not destroy infrastructure, drop databases, or change production security boundaries unless the developer names the exact operation.

Never commit secrets, `.env` files, keys, or tokens. Never print credentials. Never hardcode credentials. If a hook or scan flags a secret, redact and stop rather than working around it.

Do not execute user-controlled input on the host. User code is untrusted code.
